WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.375 --> 00:00:01.751
Hi, Jason.

00:00:01.751 --> 00:00:07.340
You've used non relational NoSQL
databases in production projects.?

00:00:07.424 --> 00:00:09.384
Hi, Martin. Yes, I have.

00:00:09.384 --> 00:00:12.387
I've learned a lot along the way
about what works well

00:00:12.595 --> 00:00:15.348
and what doesn't in some real life
applications.

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:17.058
I'll be happy to share with you.

00:00:17.058 --> 00:00:28.570
We'd love to learn from you.

00:00:28.653 --> 00:00:29.612
So, Jason,

00:00:29.612 --> 00:00:34.284
we met two years ago at developer
conferences in London and in California.

00:00:34.325 --> 00:00:39.039
I learned that you speak at conferences
about Firebase Angry Cloud,

00:00:39.122 --> 00:00:42.584
which made you a certified Google
Developer Expert,

00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:47.213
and you're also Google
Cloud certified professional architect.

00:00:47.255 --> 00:00:50.550
And you run your own business 418.dev.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.262
Yes. Martin, I always like to keep busy.

00:00:54.345 --> 00:00:58.850
Maybe this video should be about time
management instead of databases.

00:00:58.933 --> 00:01:03.188
It's sometimes hard for me to work
and pick up groceries on the same day.

00:01:03.271 --> 00:01:07.734
Well, if you enjoy something
you love, it's easier to find the time.

00:01:07.776 --> 00:01:09.569
Wise words.

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:16.701
Years ago, in my first job, they taught me
about relational databases that use SQL.

00:01:16.785 --> 00:01:21.372
The data is in tables
and those tables are linked by IDs.

00:01:21.456 --> 00:01:26.086
And any system built back in those days
used relational databases.

00:01:26.169 --> 00:01:29.756
But then non relational databases
came along.

00:01:29.798 --> 00:01:34.636
Well, with the web, we needed a way
to build more scalable applications.

00:01:34.719 --> 00:01:37.972
Non relational databases filled that need.

00:01:38.056 --> 00:01:41.351
These databases store data in documents.

00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:44.646
Applications
only need to read a single document

00:01:44.854 --> 00:01:48.900
for each entity,
which puts less load on the server

00:01:48.983 --> 00:01:52.487
so these databases can handle
a lot of traffic.

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:53.530
Got it.

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:56.324
Now you've used non relational databases

00:01:56.324 --> 00:01:59.577
in production on some major projects,
right?

00:01:59.661 --> 00:02:00.203
Right.

00:02:00.203 --> 00:02:04.833
And I've used them for everything
from simple mobile applications to large

00:02:04.833 --> 00:02:09.671
enterprise Iot projects, consuming
tens of millions of events per day.

00:02:09.754 --> 00:02:11.923
And that's what I wanted to ask, Jason.

00:02:11.923 --> 00:02:14.843
What lessons have you learned about non

00:02:14.843 --> 00:02:19.097
relational databases
in these real life projects?

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:22.600
It comes
down to three architecture decisions.

00:02:22.684 --> 00:02:26.813
When to use relational versus
non relational databases,

00:02:26.896 --> 00:02:30.358
how to design
a good non relational data model

00:02:30.441 --> 00:02:36.072
and when to choose direct client
access versus an API layer.

00:02:36.156 --> 00:02:36.906
Excellent.

00:02:36.906 --> 00:02:39.492
Let's dig into each of those.

00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:41.619
So the first decision on your list is

00:02:41.619 --> 00:02:44.455
when you use relational versus
non relational database.

00:02:44.455 --> 00:02:46.166
Tell us more about that!

00:02:46.166 --> 00:02:48.585
Well, this is a big topic,

00:02:48.585 --> 00:02:53.047
but my rule of thumb
is use a non relational database

00:02:53.131 --> 00:02:57.594
when the number of reads is much greater
than the number of fronts.

00:02:57.677 --> 00:02:59.804
Could you give us an example?

00:02:59.804 --> 00:03:04.434
So a consumer facing user interface
might be a good example.

00:03:04.475 --> 00:03:08.354
In many web apps and mobile apps,
there can be hundreds

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:11.191
or even thousands of times
more reads than writes.

00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:11.608
Mm hmm.

00:03:11.608 --> 00:03:14.819
Yeah,
I recognize that in my own app usage.

00:03:14.903 --> 00:03:18.489
If most database operations
are read operations

00:03:18.573 --> 00:03:23.369
a non relational database can be faster
and cheaper.

00:03:23.453 --> 00:03:28.041
The data is denormalized,
which means no complex joins.

00:03:28.124 --> 00:03:32.962
Those joins between tables
can lead to slow performance

00:03:33.046 --> 00:03:36.633
and increased
cost in regular relational databases. Hmm.

00:03:36.674 --> 00:03:37.342
All right.

00:03:37.342 --> 00:03:40.178
I've written some pretty hairy SQL joins

00:03:40.178 --> 00:03:43.181
in relational databases in previous jobs.

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:45.725
Also, non relational databases

00:03:45.725 --> 00:03:48.728
often require less code to integrate with.

00:03:48.728 --> 00:03:51.981
That means higher developer productivity.

00:03:52.065 --> 00:03:54.651
And there are serverless
non relational databases,

00:03:54.651 --> 00:03:58.321
which means less operations work once
the app is up and running.

00:03:58.404 --> 00:04:00.198
That sounds pretty good.

00:04:00.198 --> 00:04:02.951
Would you ever use a relational database

00:04:02.951 --> 00:04:05.703
like I was taught back in my first job?

00:04:05.703 --> 00:04:08.498
Yes. Relational databases are great

00:04:08.498 --> 00:04:11.501
for tasks where you are making many writes

00:04:11.709 --> 00:04:15.797
or if you need to run complex queries
for things like reporting.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:16.506
Okay.

00:04:16.506 --> 00:04:21.052
Let's say a non relational database
would be a good fit for my application.

00:04:21.135 --> 00:04:23.221
Which one should I choose?

00:04:23.221 --> 00:04:27.725
Well, as you can see, I'm wearing
Firebase t-shirt, so I would normally

00:04:27.725 --> 00:04:32.355
recommend Cloud Firestore,
because it's easy to deploy and maintain.

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:37.277
Plus, the Firebase SDK is really easy
to work with and the Firebase team

00:04:37.277 --> 00:04:40.905
have some great tutorials
on their YouTube channel.

00:04:40.989 --> 00:04:43.866
There are other non relational databases
in Google Cloud

00:04:43.866 --> 00:04:48.162
platform like BigTable and Firestore
Datastore Mode,

00:04:48.246 --> 00:04:52.208
but Firestore Native Mode is a good choice
for most applications.

00:04:52.292 --> 00:04:53.710
Very good.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:58.589
Now the second decision in your list
is about data model design.

00:04:58.673 --> 00:05:02.844
Now I'm pretty good at designing
regular relational data models,

00:05:02.927 --> 00:05:06.889
but how do you design
a non relational data model?

00:05:06.973 --> 00:05:10.310
Well, first,
use the non relational database

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:14.689
as it's intended with collections
and documents.

00:05:14.772 --> 00:05:17.775
Put enough data into each document

00:05:17.775 --> 00:05:21.654
that you won't need
to fetch multiple documents.

00:05:21.738 --> 00:05:27.201
If you use a relational data model
with entities linked by IDs, you require

00:05:27.201 --> 00:05:32.665
more read operations which will increase
complexity, latency and cost.

00:05:32.707 --> 00:05:34.000
Got it.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.421
How do we decide what fields belong in
each document?

00:05:38.504 --> 00:05:43.426
My rule of thumb is the data
model should follow the user interface.

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:44.594
Got it.

00:05:44.594 --> 00:05:46.888
The data model should follow the UI.

00:05:46.888 --> 00:05:49.432
That's a simple rule to remember.

00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:52.018
Good roles usually are.

00:05:52.018 --> 00:05:55.938
But I may have data
that appears in multiple places.

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:58.983
For example,
I might look at a vehicle document

00:05:58.983 --> 00:06:03.196
and want to see the address of the garage
where it's parked overnight.

00:06:03.279 --> 00:06:07.784
But this same address would also be
in other car documents.

00:06:07.867 --> 00:06:10.828
Where should I store that common address?

00:06:10.828 --> 00:06:15.166
In a relational database,
each table holds a single instance

00:06:15.166 --> 00:06:18.711
of some data and they are connected
together with joins.

00:06:18.795 --> 00:06:23.299
That makes it easy to write a field,
but it adds load to the server

00:06:23.299 --> 00:06:28.721
with every single read
because multiple tables have to be joined

00:06:28.805 --> 00:06:31.474
in a non relational database

00:06:31.474 --> 00:06:37.271
we can save on processing time
and cost by replicating the data.

00:06:37.355 --> 00:06:39.607
So to answer your question,

00:06:39.607 --> 00:06:43.069
we copied the address
to each vehicle's document.

00:06:43.152 --> 00:06:45.571
We call this denormalization.

00:06:45.571 --> 00:06:48.157
I see how that would make for fast reads.

00:06:48.157 --> 00:06:49.992
You only read one document.

00:06:49.992 --> 00:06:53.204
But what happens
when we need to update that address?

00:06:53.287 --> 00:06:56.958
Do we have to change it
in every single document? Yes.

00:06:57.208 --> 00:07:01.754
We will have to update every document
where this data appears.

00:07:01.838 --> 00:07:05.842
And how is that efficient to update it in
multiple places?

00:07:05.925 --> 00:07:09.554
Well, Martin,
as we discussed earlier in an application

00:07:09.554 --> 00:07:13.015
where the number of reads far outweighs
the number of rights,

00:07:13.099 --> 00:07:16.936
we have to make a tradeoff
When we denormalize,

00:07:17.019 --> 00:07:20.690
the data must be copied
whenever an update is made.

00:07:20.773 --> 00:07:23.693
However, Cloud Firestore
makes this really easy

00:07:23.693 --> 00:07:27.864
for us
using update triggers and Cloud Functions.

00:07:27.905 --> 00:07:29.907
I like Cloud Functions.

00:07:29.907 --> 00:07:31.993
Tell me how you designed this!

00:07:31.993 --> 00:07:36.080
So when you update a document
in Firestore, it can trigger a Cloud

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:37.457
Function.

00:07:37.457 --> 00:07:41.544
That Cloud Function can read the updated
garage address,

00:07:41.586 --> 00:07:45.673
and update each of the vehicle's documents

00:07:45.756 --> 00:07:48.759
in that garage with the new address.

00:07:48.843 --> 00:07:49.635
Great.

00:07:49.635 --> 00:07:54.098
I like that the trigger does
this automatically so the app developer

00:07:54.098 --> 00:07:58.311
doesn't have to remember to do it
every time they update a document.

00:07:58.394 --> 00:08:01.981
Now, your third
design decision from your list,

00:08:02.064 --> 00:08:06.736
that is to choose
direct client access versus an API layer.

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:08.696
What does that mean?

00:08:08.696 --> 00:08:12.909
Well, there are two ways
that client apps can access Firestore:

00:08:12.992 --> 00:08:16.996
through a server side
API layer that you build yourself

00:08:17.079 --> 00:08:21.083
or directly from the client
using the Firebase SDK.

00:08:21.125 --> 00:08:21.834
Right.

00:08:21.834 --> 00:08:26.130
I have accessed Firestore directly
from a web client, in a previous project.

00:08:26.214 --> 00:08:30.134
I avoided writing a lot of server side
code that way!

00:08:30.218 --> 00:08:34.805
That's one great advantage
of accessing Firestore from the client.

00:08:34.889 --> 00:08:37.266
If you have to write less code,

00:08:37.266 --> 00:08:39.727
you will get your app to market sooner.

00:08:39.727 --> 00:08:44.732
Also, less code means lower maintenance
costs and hopefully fewer bugs.

00:08:44.815 --> 00:08:48.486
And there are Firestore SDKs for Web, iOS,

00:08:48.694 --> 00:08:51.489
Android, Flutter and Unity.

00:08:51.489 --> 00:08:52.240
That's right.

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:55.576
It takes a lot of work
to build a production API

00:08:55.576 --> 00:08:58.788
that can handle things
like spotty internet connections,

00:08:58.871 --> 00:09:04.335
caching, attacks, load balancing,
authentication and authorization.

00:09:04.418 --> 00:09:08.798
The Firebase SDKs take care of all of that
for you.

00:09:08.881 --> 00:09:09.465
Right.

00:09:09.465 --> 00:09:13.219
Just last week I had to build an API
that implemented paging

00:09:13.302 --> 00:09:16.389
and just a simple thing like that, is

00:09:16.389 --> 00:09:19.892
pretty complex
to test it and write all the code for it.

00:09:19.976 --> 00:09:22.436
I wouldn't want to have to build
all of the other things

00:09:22.436 --> 00:09:25.064
you mentioned for every API I build.

00:09:25.064 --> 00:09:25.982
Yes, indeed.

00:09:25.982 --> 00:09:30.820
And another great advantage of direct
client access with the Firebase SDK is

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:35.866
that clients can be notified in real time
when your data changes in Firestore.

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:38.911
So let's revisit our fleet management app.

00:09:38.911 --> 00:09:43.624
If each car has a GPS device
and reports its location,

00:09:43.708 --> 00:09:48.337
you could display a map
that shows all the cars in real time.

00:09:48.421 --> 00:09:51.424
Whenever a car's position is updated
by Firestore,

00:09:51.632 --> 00:09:55.761
it will then push the update
to all web and mobile apps

00:09:55.845 --> 00:09:59.974
so they can adjust that car's position
on the map in real time.

00:10:00.057 --> 00:10:01.475
Very nice.

00:10:01.475 --> 00:10:06.939
But if users are accessing the database
directly, how do you handle authorization?

00:10:07.023 --> 00:10:09.942
If you use the Firebase SDK, you define

00:10:09.942 --> 00:10:13.904
security rules
and these handle the authorization.

00:10:13.988 --> 00:10:16.240
The rules tell Firestore who is allowed

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:19.577
to read
or write each document in the database

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:23.205
based on the authentication token
that they get at login.

00:10:23.289 --> 00:10:26.292
So let's go back to our fleet management
app example.

00:10:26.375 --> 00:10:28.961
We want to keep financial details
of the car

00:10:28.961 --> 00:10:33.049
in the database,
like maybe the purchase price and so on.

00:10:33.132 --> 00:10:37.470
If the fields in the main
vehicle document, whoever can read

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:41.807
that document, can also read
those financial related fields.

00:10:41.891 --> 00:10:43.184
By putting the finance

00:10:43.184 --> 00:10:47.146
data in a separate document
in a sub collection

00:10:47.146 --> 00:10:50.358
of that vehicle document
we can limit access to it.

00:10:50.441 --> 00:10:51.734
Got it.

00:10:51.734 --> 00:10:55.071
Direct client access sounds pretty great.

00:10:55.154 --> 00:10:58.449
Do you use it
in all your applications, Jason?

00:10:58.532 --> 00:11:01.535
Yes, but not for all use cases.

00:11:01.535 --> 00:11:04.413
In our example above,
we were able to restrict

00:11:04.413 --> 00:11:08.334
client access
using Firestore security rules.

00:11:08.376 --> 00:11:11.295
But let's say we want to expose this data
to a third

00:11:11.295 --> 00:11:14.965
party application
with a custom data model.

00:11:15.049 --> 00:11:18.844
For that we can create a server side API

00:11:18.928 --> 00:11:22.473
which will bundle this data
into a single response.

00:11:22.556 --> 00:11:26.394
And by providing that API layer,
you make sure that both

00:11:26.394 --> 00:11:30.231
your clients
apps and third parties can call it?

00:11:30.314 --> 00:11:31.524
That's right.

00:11:31.524 --> 00:11:37.613
With an API layer, it's also easier
to optimize performance and cost.

00:11:37.697 --> 00:11:38.781
Plus, you can

00:11:38.781 --> 00:11:42.785
remap the data model
to suit your needs, perform

00:11:42.785 --> 00:11:47.790
maybe some simple aggregations or even
combine it with data from other sources.

00:11:47.873 --> 00:11:51.585
But there's nothing to stop
you using a mix of direct client access

00:11:51.669 --> 00:11:54.547
and API access in the same application.

00:11:54.547 --> 00:11:55.673
All right.

00:11:55.673 --> 00:11:56.590
Let's recap.

00:11:56.590 --> 00:12:00.261
We talked about
when to use a non relational database.

00:12:00.344 --> 00:12:03.347
Two, how to design a good data model
for it.

00:12:03.431 --> 00:12:07.768
And three, when to use direct database
access from clients.

00:12:07.852 --> 00:12:11.063
Thanks for sharing your expertise
with us, Jason!

00:12:11.105 --> 00:12:12.189
Happy to share.

00:12:12.189 --> 00:12:16.235
I've seen non relational databases
increase productivity

00:12:16.235 --> 00:12:20.072
and performance
in many of my customers’ systems.

00:12:20.156 --> 00:12:23.826
And where can viewers get more details
on how to create an application

00:12:23.826 --> 00:12:25.244
with Firestore?

00:12:25.244 --> 00:12:28.205
I know just the person to ask.

00:12:28.372 --> 00:12:32.668
Hey, Puf, Martin and I have just been
chatting about Cloud Firestore.

00:12:32.752 --> 00:12:35.504
Can you tell people where they can get
started?

00:12:35.504 --> 00:12:38.299
Hey, Jason. And sure I can. Hi, Martin.

00:12:38.299 --> 00:12:41.427
You can find lots and loads of resources
on the Firebase YouTube channel

00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:46.223
and at firebase.google.com where there are
many fantastic guides and tutorials.

00:12:46.265 --> 00:12:48.934
Thanks, Puf. It was great to see you.

00:12:48.934 --> 00:12:52.688
You too, Jason, and thanks, Martin,
for letting me gatecrash your show!

00:12:52.772 --> 00:12:55.191
No problem. You're always welcome.

00:12:55.191 --> 00:12:57.735
And thank you, everyone for watching.

00:12:57.735 --> 00:13:01.405
If you have questions for Jason
or me, please, I had a comment below.

00:13:01.489 --> 00:13:02.740
Also, let me know

00:13:02.740 --> 00:13:07.286
if there are other serverless topics
you'd like to see in future episodes.

00:13:07.328 --> 00:13:10.331
I read every single comment.

00:13:10.539 --> 00:13:11.791
Until next time!

